window.LAB_ITEMS = {"version":1,"lab":2,"course":"Phonetics","title":"The Speech Machine","zodiac":{"index":2,"animal":"ox","label":"Ox"},"unit":{"one":"Trail","many":"trails"},"shuffle_items":true,"lede":"You will sort the parts of the speech machine into its three systems, place every part on the map of the vocal tract, and build ten words out of their sounds.","grading":{"total":30,"full_threshold":20,"full_score":100,"partial_score":50,"zero_score":0,"full_points":2,"partial_points":1,"alt_credit":0.5,"round":"up"},"legs":[{"id":"ss","n":1,"title":"System Sorter","accent":"amber","subtitle":"Sort each part of the speech machine into its system","concept":"Speech is made by one machine with three systems. The <b>respiratory system</b> is the power supply: it pushes the air. The <b>laryngeal system</b> is the sound source: it turns that air into a buzz. The <b>supralaryngeal system</b> is everything above the larynx: the resonating chambers of the throat, mouth and nose, and the tongue, lips and jaw that shape the buzz into speech.","prompt":"Tap the system this part belongs to.","choices":[{"v":"resp","label":"Respiratory","tag":"power supply","icon":"resp"},{"v":"lar","label":"Laryngeal","tag":"sound source","icon":"lar"},{"v":"supra","label":"Supralaryngeal","tag":"resonating chambers","icon":"supra"}],"items":[{"id":"ss01","type":"choice","word":"Lungs","answer":"resp","explain":"The lungs are the power supply. Speech runs on a slow, controlled outbreath: air stored in the lungs is pushed upward, and everything the rest of the machine does, it does to that moving air.","miss":{"lar":"The lungs make no sound of their own. Air leaving them is still silent, and stays silent until it reaches the vocal folds higher up.","supra":"The lungs sit at the very bottom of the machine. The resonating chambers are the spaces above the voice, not the pump below it."},"accept":{},"note":null},{"id":"ss02","type":"choice","word":"Trachea","answer":"resp","accept":{"lar":"You put the trachea with the larynx, and they really are joined: the larynx sits directly on top of the trachea. But the trachea is the air pipe <b>below</b> the larynx, so it belongs to the respiratory system."},"note":"The larynx is the dividing line. Everything below it, including the trachea, is respiratory. The larynx, with the vocal folds inside it, is laryngeal. Everything above it is supralaryngeal.","explain":"The trachea, the windpipe, is the tube that carries air from the lungs up toward the larynx. It moves air and does nothing else, which makes it respiratory.","miss":{"supra":"The trachea sits below the larynx, and supralaryngeal means above the larynx. The trachea is on the air-supply side of that line."}},{"id":"ss03","type":"choice","word":"Diaphragm","answer":"resp","explain":"The diaphragm is the broad, dome-shaped muscle under the lungs. It contracts to pull air in, then relaxes slowly and steadily so the air can flow out through a whole spoken phrase on one breath.","miss":{"lar":"The diaphragm never touches the sound itself. It works under the lungs, well below the larynx where the voice is made.","supra":"The diaphragm is the muscle under the lungs, about as far from the mouth and nose as the machine goes."},"accept":{},"note":null},{"id":"ss04","type":"choice","word":"Vocal folds","answer":"lar","explain":"The vocal folds are the sound source itself. Air from the lungs sets them vibrating hundreds of times a second, and that buzz is the raw material of every voiced sound you make.","miss":{"resp":"The folds do not push the air, they ride in it. The airstream is made below them; the folds are what turns it into sound.","supra":"The word itself rules this out: <i>supra</i>laryngeal means above the larynx, and the vocal folds sit inside the larynx."},"accept":{},"note":null},{"id":"ss05","type":"choice","word":"Tongue","answer":"supra","explain":"The tongue is the busiest shaper in the machine. Sitting in the mouth, above the vocal folds, it molds the buzz into one vowel or consonant after another.","miss":{"resp":"The tongue moves no air. It works on sound that already exists, up in the mouth.","lar":"The tongue shapes the sound, it does not make it. The voice is born lower down; the tongue meets it in the mouth."},"accept":{},"note":null},{"id":"ss06","type":"choice","word":"Lips","answer":"supra","explain":"The lips are the last stop before sound leaves the body. Closing them makes sounds like /p/, /b/ and /m/; rounding them shapes vowels like /u/.","miss":{"resp":"The lips are the exit, not the pump. By the time air reaches them it has already been through the whole machine.","lar":"The lips shape the sound at the very top of the machine, a long way above the vocal folds where the voice is made."},"accept":{},"note":null},{"id":"ss07","type":"choice","word":"Oral cavity","answer":"supra","explain":"The oral cavity, the mouth, is the main resonating chamber. Every movement of the tongue, jaw and lips changes its shape, and every change of shape changes the sound.","miss":{"resp":"Air passes through the mouth on the way out, but the mouth's job is resonance: it colors the sound rather than pushing the air.","lar":"The mouth is a chamber above the larynx, not part of the voice box itself."},"accept":{},"note":null},{"id":"ss08","type":"choice","word":"Nasal cavity","answer":"supra","explain":"The nasal cavity is the resonating chamber behind the nose. For sounds like /m/ and /n/ the air is routed through it, and that is what gives them their humming quality.","miss":{"resp":"You do breathe through it, but in speech the nasal cavity's job is resonance: it is a chamber the sound rings in, above the vocal folds.","lar":"The nose is nowhere near the larynx. It is the upper chamber of the supralaryngeal system."},"accept":{},"note":null},{"id":"ss09","type":"choice","word":"Pharynx","answer":"supra","accept":{"lar":"You put the pharynx with the larynx, and they are next-door neighbors with nearly twin names. But the pharynx is the stretch of throat <b>above</b> the larynx, and above the larynx means supralaryngeal."},"explain":"The pharynx is the open throat: the tube connecting the larynx to the mouth and nose. It is the first resonating chamber the buzz passes through on its way up.","miss":{"resp":"Air does pass through the pharynx, but so does sound: it is a resonating space above the vocal folds, which places it with the chambers."},"note":null},{"id":"ss10","type":"choice","word":"Jaw","answer":"supra","explain":"The jaw is the frame the other shapers ride on. It carries the tongue, the lower teeth and the lower lip. Lowering or raising it resizes the mouth's resonating space, which helps separate low vowels like the one in <i>father</i> from high vowels like the one in <i>see</i>.","miss":{"resp":"The jaw moves the mouth, not the air. Its work is all above the vocal folds.","lar":"The jaw sits above and in front of the larynx, framing the mouth. It shapes the sound rather than making it."},"accept":{},"note":null}]},{"id":"pw","n":2,"title":"Pathway","accent":"indigo","subtitle":"Place every stop on the road from the lungs to the lips","concept":"Air travels one road when you speak: up out of the lungs, through the vibrating vocal folds, and on through the throat to the mouth and nose.","prompt":"Tap the right spot on the diagram.","regions":[{"v":"lungs","label":"Lungs"},{"v":"trachea","label":"Trachea"},{"v":"larynx","label":"Larynx"},{"v":"vocal_folds","label":"Vocal folds"},{"v":"pharynx","label":"Pharynx"},{"v":"nasal_cavity","label":"Nasal cavity"},{"v":"oral_cavity","label":"Oral cavity"},{"v":"tongue","label":"Tongue"},{"v":"hard_palate","label":"Hard palate"},{"v":"lips","label":"Lips"}],"items":[{"id":"pw01","type":"spot","word":"Lungs","answer":"lungs","explain":"The road starts in the lungs. They fill the chest, and the slow squeeze of the breath out of them is what powers everything that follows.","miss":{"trachea":"That is the windpipe, the tube the air travels through. The lungs are the two large organs it leads down to."},"accept":{},"note":null},{"id":"pw02","type":"spot","word":"Trachea","answer":"trachea","explain":"From the lungs the air climbs the trachea, the windpipe: a straight tube held open by rings of cartilage, running up the front of the neck.","miss":{"pharynx":"The pharynx is also a tube, but it is the stretch of throat above the voice box. The trachea is the ringed pipe below it, heading down to the lungs.","larynx":"The larynx is the box at the top of this pipe. The trachea is the pipe itself."},"accept":{},"note":null},{"id":"pw03","type":"spot","word":"Larynx","answer":"larynx","accept":{"vocal_folds":"You pointed inside the right structure: the vocal folds live inside the larynx. The larynx label belongs to the whole cartilage box at the top of the windpipe, not just the folds within it."},"explain":"At the top of the trachea sits the larynx, the voice box: a housing of cartilage you can feel from outside as the bump of the Adam's apple.","miss":{"pharynx":"Nearly twin names, different places: the pharynx is the open throat above. The larynx is the cartilage box sitting on top of the windpipe."},"note":null},{"id":"pw04","type":"spot","word":"Vocal folds","answer":"vocal_folds","accept":{"larynx":"Right neighborhood: the folds are inside the larynx. This label asks for the folds themselves, the two small shelves of muscle stretched across the middle of that box."},"explain":"Inside the larynx, the airstream squeezes between the two vocal folds and sets them vibrating. This is the exact spot where air becomes sound.","miss":{"trachea":"Lower than the folds: that is the windpipe carrying still-silent air. The folds are up inside the voice box."},"note":null},{"id":"pw05","type":"spot","word":"Pharynx","answer":"pharynx","explain":"Above the larynx the buzz rises through the pharynx, the open column of the throat behind the tongue. It is the corridor connecting the voice to the mouth and nose.","miss":{"larynx":"One landmark too low: the larynx is the voice box itself. The pharynx is the throat above it, running up behind the mouth.","oral_cavity":"The oral cavity is the mouth. The pharynx is the vertical passage behind it, connecting the larynx upward."},"accept":{},"note":null},{"id":"pw06","type":"spot","word":"Nasal cavity","answer":"nasal_cavity","explain":"One of the airflow's two exits. For nasal sounds the passage into the nose is open, and the sound rings through this large chamber above the roof of the mouth.","miss":{"oral_cavity":"That is the mouth. The nasal cavity is the chamber above it, behind the nose."},"accept":{},"note":null},{"id":"pw07","type":"spot","word":"Oral cavity","answer":"oral_cavity","explain":"The busier of the airflow's two exits: the open space of the mouth, where most speech sounds get their final shape.","miss":{"nasal_cavity":"That chamber is behind the nose. The oral cavity is the open space of the mouth, below the palate."},"accept":{},"note":null},{"id":"pw08","type":"spot","word":"Tongue","answer":"tongue","explain":"Filling the floor of the mouth, the tongue arches, bunches, taps and glides to carve one sound after another out of the passing voice.","miss":{"oral_cavity":"Close: the oral cavity is the open space. The tongue is the large muscle filling the floor of that space."},"accept":{},"note":null},{"id":"pw09","type":"spot","word":"Hard palate","answer":"hard_palate","explain":"The bony roof of the mouth, a firm surface for the tongue to work against. The front of the tongue rises toward it for sounds like the <b>y</b> in <i>yes</i>.","miss":{"oral_cavity":"The oral cavity is the open space of the mouth. The hard palate is its bony ceiling.","tongue":"Other side of the gap: the tongue rises from the floor of the mouth, and the hard palate is the roof it presses toward."},"accept":{},"note":null},{"id":"pw10","type":"spot","word":"Lips","answer":"lips","explain":"The last stop on the airflow's road. The lips close, open and round to finish the sound, and past them the word is out in the world.","miss":{"tongue":"The tongue does its shaping inside the mouth. The lips are the very front door."},"accept":{},"note":null}]},{"id":"wb","n":3,"title":"Word Builder","accent":"magenta","subtitle":"Build each word out of its sounds","concept":"Every spoken word is a chain of phonemes. Here you build the chain yourself: listen, then stamp the sounds into the boxes in order. The number of boxes tells you how many sounds the word has; your job is which sounds, in which order.","audio_note":"<b>Tap any tile to hear its sound.</b> A consonant is hard to hear on its own, so most of them are recorded with a vowel after: /b/ plays as <i>ba</i>. The vowel is only there to carry the sound, so listen to the front of the syllable.","prompt":"Tap the sounds in order to build the word. Tap a filled box to take one out.","tiles":[{"ipa":"b","slug":"b","cat":"c"},{"ipa":"g","slug":"g","cat":"c"},{"ipa":"k","slug":"k","cat":"c"},{"ipa":"l","slug":"l","cat":"c"},{"ipa":"m","slug":"m","cat":"c"},{"ipa":"s","slug":"s","cat":"c"},{"ipa":"t","slug":"t","cat":"c"},{"ipa":"ʃ","slug":"sh","cat":"c"},{"ipa":"i","slug":"iy","cat":"v"},{"ipa":"u","slug":"uw","cat":"v"},{"ipa":"eɪ","slug":"ey","cat":"v"},{"ipa":"oʊ","slug":"ow","cat":"v"}],"items":[{"id":"wb01","type":"build","word":"me","audio":"w_me","answer":"m.iy","chips":[{"letters":"m","ipa":"m","slug":"m"},{"letters":"e","ipa":"i","slug":"iy"}],"explain":"Two letters, two sounds: /m/ then /i/. The letter <b>e</b> is spelling the /i/ sound here, the same sound <b>ee</b> spells in <i>bee</i>.","note":null,"accept":{},"miss":{}},{"id":"wb02","type":"build","word":"shoe","audio":"w_shoe","answer":"sh.uw","chips":[{"letters":"sh","ipa":"ʃ","slug":"sh"},{"letters":"oe","ipa":"u","slug":"uw"}],"explain":"Four letters, two sounds. <b>sh</b> spells the single sound /ʃ/, and <b>oe</b> spells /u/.","note":null,"accept":{},"miss":{"s.uw":"That build starts with /s/, but <b>sh</b> spells a different phoneme altogether: /ʃ/, one sound made further back in the mouth."}},{"id":"wb03","type":"build","word":"go","audio":"w_go","answer":"g.ow","chips":[{"letters":"g","ipa":"g","slug":"g"},{"letters":"o","ipa":"oʊ","slug":"ow"}],"explain":"The letter <b>o</b> spells /oʊ/, a vowel that glides from one position toward another.","note":"A gliding vowel like /oʊ/ or /eɪ/ is written with two symbols joined by a bar, but it is one phoneme, so it takes one tile.","accept":{},"miss":{}},{"id":"wb04","type":"build","word":"say","audio":"w_say","answer":"s.ey","chips":[{"letters":"s","ipa":"s","slug":"s"},{"letters":"ay","ipa":"eɪ","slug":"ey"}],"explain":"/s/ then the gliding vowel /eɪ/. The two letters <b>ay</b> spell that one vowel together.","note":null,"accept":{},"miss":{}},{"id":"wb05","type":"build","word":"key","audio":"w_key","answer":"k.iy","chips":[{"letters":"k","ipa":"k","slug":"k"},{"letters":"ey","ipa":"i","slug":"iy"}],"explain":"/k/ then /i/. The letters <b>ey</b> spell plain /i/ in this word.","note":null,"accept":{},"miss":{"k.ey":"The spelling <b>ey</b> looks like the glide /eɪ/ in <i>say</i>, but in <i>key</i> those letters spell /i/. Trust your ear over the letters."}},{"id":"wb06","type":"build","word":"boat","audio":"w_boat","answer":"b.ow.t","chips":[{"letters":"b","ipa":"b","slug":"b"},{"letters":"oa","ipa":"oʊ","slug":"ow"},{"letters":"t","ipa":"t","slug":"t"}],"explain":"Four letters, three sounds: the pair <b>oa</b> spells the single gliding vowel /oʊ/.","note":null,"accept":{},"miss":{}},{"id":"wb07","type":"build","word":"lace","audio":"w_lace","answer":"l.ey.s","chips":[{"letters":"l","ipa":"l","slug":"l"},{"letters":"a","ipa":"eɪ","slug":"ey"},{"letters":"ce","ipa":"s","slug":"s"}],"explain":"The <b>a</b> spells the glide /eɪ/, and <b>ce</b> spells /s/. The final <b>e</b> is silent: it changes how the vowel is read, but adds no sound of its own.","note":null,"accept":{},"miss":{"l.ey.k":"The letter <b>c</b> often spells /k/, but before <b>e</b> it spells /s/, as it does here. <i>Lace</i> ends in the same sound as <i>less</i>."}},{"id":"wb08","type":"build","word":"loose","audio":"w_loose","answer":"l.uw.s","chips":[{"letters":"l","ipa":"l","slug":"l"},{"letters":"oo","ipa":"u","slug":"uw"},{"letters":"se","ipa":"s","slug":"s"}],"explain":"Five letters, three sounds. <b>oo</b> spells /u/, and <b>se</b> spells /s/. Said with a /z/ instead, it would be a different word: <i>lose</i>.","note":null,"accept":{},"miss":{}},{"id":"wb09","type":"build","word":"toast","audio":"w_toast","answer":"t.ow.s.t","chips":[{"letters":"t","ipa":"t","slug":"t"},{"letters":"oa","ipa":"oʊ","slug":"ow"},{"letters":"s","ipa":"s","slug":"s"},{"letters":"t","ipa":"t","slug":"t"}],"explain":"/t/ appears at both ends: a tile can be used as often as its sound occurs. In the middle, <b>oa</b> spells the one gliding vowel /oʊ/.","note":null,"accept":{},"miss":{}},{"id":"wb10","type":"build","word":"steam","audio":"w_steam","answer":"s.t.iy.m","chips":[{"letters":"s","ipa":"s","slug":"s"},{"letters":"t","ipa":"t","slug":"t"},{"letters":"ea","ipa":"i","slug":"iy"},{"letters":"m","ipa":"m","slug":"m"}],"explain":"Four sounds in a row: /s/, /t/, /i/, /m/. The pair <b>ea</b> spells plain /i/.","note":null,"accept":{},"miss":{"s.t.ey.m":"The letters <b>ea</b> look like they might glide, but in <i>steam</i> they spell plain /i/, the vowel of <i>see</i>."}}]}]};
